{"records":[{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"sgdcys9b82","title":"Mycobacterium-Host Screening Identifies Novel Lysosomal Escape Pathways","description":"This dataset explores intracellular survival mechanisms of Mycobacterium leprae. Using clinical lesion proteomics, we identified 14 highly expressed T7SS effectors. Genome-wide yeast two-/three-hybrid screening against a macrophage interactome revealed 132 host targets and mapped the first global M. leprae effector–host interaction network. Functional validation defined two conserved lysosomal escape pathways: PPE68–Coro1A preventing phagosome–lysosome fusion, and EsxA–EsxB–CCDC115 disrupting V-ATPase assembly. Comparative M. marinum models confirmed their essential role in survival and pathogenesis. The dataset includes proteomic profiles, interaction matrices, and validation assays, which can be used to investigate host–pathogen interactions and identify therapeutic targets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-29T07:59:18.944Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/sgdcys9b82","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/sgdcys9b82"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Bingqing","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"xiaojie","last_name":"Liao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yongyu","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Longyun ","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rongxian","last_name":"Xie","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Haihong","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuzhen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xiaoyu","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nannan","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"玮玮","last_name":"王","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhongrui","last_name":"Ma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yinjie","last_name":"Lin","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Peidian","last_name":"Shi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wenchao","last_name":"Li","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yuanqiang","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhenzhen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xuechao","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jianwen","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zhe","last_name":"Wang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Zihao","last_name":"Mi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hong","last_name":"Liu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Furen","last_name":"Zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology","Bacterial Pathogenesis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"54v7hn8hvz","title":"Legionella pneumophila exploits the endo-lysosomal network for phagosome biogenesis","description":"L. pneumophila strains harboring wild-type rpsL such as Lp02rpsLWT cannot replicate in mouse bone marrow-derived macrophages (BMDMs) due to induction of extensive lysosome damage and apoptosis. The mechanism of this unique infection-induced cell death remains unknown. Using a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screening, we identified Hmg20a and Nol9 as host factors important for restricting strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Depletion of Hmg20a protects macrophages from infection-induced lysosomal damage and apoptosis, allowing productive bacterial replication. The restriction imposed by Hmg20a was mediated by repressing the expression of several endo-lysosomal proteins, including the small GTPase Rab7. We found that SUMOylated Rab7 is recruited to the bacterial phagosome via SulF, a Dot/Icm effector that harbors a SUMO-interacting motif (SIM). Moreover, overexpression of Rab7 rescues intracellular growth of strain Lp02rpsLWT in BMDMs. Our results establish that L. pneumophila exploits the lysosomal network for the biogenesis of its phagosome in BMDMs.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-09-07T16:49:43.441Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/54v7hn8hvz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/54v7hn8hvz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Chuang","last_name":"Li","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microbiology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"s3jtdhjdbs","title":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Protein import motor complex reacts to mitochondrial misfolding by reducing protein import and activating mitophagy'","description":"Supplementary data for Michaelis et al., 'Mitochondrial import loss induces mitophagy without membrane depolarization'. We hypothesized that inhibition of mitochondrial protein import induced mitophagy independent of mitochondrial depolarization. This dataset contains raw Illumina sequencing data for all samples from a genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 screen for mitophagy regulators. The genome-wide library is Brunello from Doench et al Nat Biotechnol. 2016. We found that the pre-sequence translocase-associated motor complex PAM controls mitophagy induction. Loss of PAM caused a loss in protein import and was sufficient to induce mitophagy without depolarization.\nThe samples have been sequenced on an Illumina NextSeq 500 in single end mode with 75+8 cycles. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-05T15:38:53.752Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/s3jtdhjdbs","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/s3jtdhjdbs"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Michaelis","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Melinda","last_name":"Brunstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Süleyman","last_name":"Bozkurt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ludovico","last_name":"Alves","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Wegner","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Manuel","last_name":"Kaulich","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Pohl","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Christian","last_name":"Münch","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Proteomics","Mitochondrion","Mitophagy","Genetic Screening","Next Generation Sequencing","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"bkxfv6yzn5","title":"ARMH3-mediated recruitment of Phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase beta directs Golgi-to-endosome trafficking and activation of the antiviral effector STING","description":"The cGAS-STING pathway mediates cytoplasmic DNA-triggered innate immunity. STING activation is initiated by cyclic-GMP-AMP (cGAMP)-induced translocation from the endoplasmic reticulum and sulfated glycosaminoglycans-induced polymerization at the Golgi. Here we examined the mechanisms underlying STING transport and activation beyond the Golgi. A genome-wide CRISPR–Cas9 screen identified Armadillo-like helical domain-containing protein 3 (ARMH3) as critical for STING activation. Upon cGAMP-triggered translocation, ARMH3 interacted with STING at the Golgi and recruited phosphatidylinositol 4-kinase beta (PI4KB) to synthesize PI4P, which directed STING Golgi-to-endosome trafficking via PI4P-binding proteins AP-1 and GGA2. Disrupting PI4P-dependent lipid transport through RNAi of other PI4P-binding proteins impaired STING activation. Consistently, disturbed lipid composition inhibited STING activation whereas aberrantly elevated cellular PI4P led to cGAS-independent STING activation. Armh3fl/fllLyzCre/Cre mice were susceptible to DNA virus challenge in vivo. Thus, ARMH3 bridges STING and PIK4B to generate PI4P for STING transportation and activation, an interaction conserved in all eukaryotes.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-02-20T13:53:07.979Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/bkxfv6yzn5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/bkxfv6yzn5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengfan","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"run","last_name":"fang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Qifei","last_name":"Jiang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Xinying","last_name":"Jia","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Cytokines","Phosphoinositides","Innate Immunity"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cdgx63gd5y","title":"TRIM21 induces selective autophagy of viruses and bacteria","description":"TRIM21 is an exceptionally versatile ubiquitin ligase that can be directed by antibodies to target oligomeric protein scaffolds, viral capsids, and proteopathic aggregates for intracellular degradation. How the cell degrades these typically resistant substrates remains poorly understood. To address this, we used TRIM21 viral restriction to create a genome-wide phenotypic screen for antibody-dependent capsid degradation. We identify an antimicrobial selective macroautophagy pathway in mammalian cells, which we term \"antibody-directed xenophagy.\" We show this mechanism restricts structurally diverse pathogens, including adenovirus and Salmonella. Using quantitative microscopy, we demonstrate that TRIM21 rapidly intercepts antibody-pathogen complexes, leading to ubiquitin ligase activation. Following this, selective autophagy adaptors are recruited and viral cargoes are delivered to lysosomes. This process reduces Salmonella pathology and bacterial tissue invasion in mice. We propose that TRIM21 evolved through competition with pathogens to induce autophagy of diverse and complex substrates, potentially explaining its versatility for targeted protein degradation.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-05-29T20:32:28.807Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cdgx63gd5y","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cdgx63gd5y"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"Rhinesmith","orcid":"0000-0002-9983-0287"}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/03x94j517","name":"Medical Research Council","grant_id":"U105181010"},{"id":"https://ror.org/029chgv08","name":"Wellcome Trust","grant_id":"223054/Z/21/Z"}],"categories":["Protein Degradation","Autophagy","Host Immune Response","Antiviral Immunity","Bacterial Host Response","Ubiquitin Ligase"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"d5vw9sjcrv","title":"A target expression threshold dictates invader defense  and autoimmunity by CRISPR-Cas13","description":"Immune systems must recognize and clear foreign invaders without eliciting autoimmunity. CRISPR-Cas immune systems in prokaryotes manage this task by following two criteria: extensive guide:target complementarity and a defined target-flanking motif. Here we report an additional requirement for RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas13 systems: expression of the target transcript exceeding a threshold. This finding is based on targeting endogenous non-essential transcripts, which rarely elicited dormancy through collateral RNA degradation. Instead, eliciting dormancy required over-expressing targeted transcripts above a threshold. A genome-wide screen confirmed target expression levels as the principal determinant of cytotoxic autoimmunity and revealed that the threshold shifts with the guide:target pair. This expression threshold ensured defense against a lytic bacteriophage yet allowed tolerance of a targeted beneficial gene expressed from an invading plasmid. These findings establish target expression levels as a third criterion for immune defense by RNA-targeting CRISPR-Cas systems, buffering against autoimmunity and distinguishing pathogenic and benign invaders.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-06-08T11:55:48.983Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/d5vw9sjcrv","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/d5vw9sjcrv"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elena","last_name":"Vialetto","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yanying","last_name":"Yu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Collins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Katharina","last_name":"Wandera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lars","last_name":"Barquist","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chase","last_name":"Beisel","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Data Analysis","Data Access"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":142983,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":159,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":142983,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":142206,"restricted":70,"embargoed":707},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2358},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1404},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":698},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":482},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":456},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":441},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":424},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":396},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":335},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":283}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":140314},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1914},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":140},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":81},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":26}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":906},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":884},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":534},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":374}]}}